I found the bug. In the `stsz_box_read` function, in the compact sizes (stz2) path:

```c
for (i = 0; i < ptr->sampleCount; ) {
    switch (ptr->sampleSize) {
    case 4:
        ptr->sizes[i] = gf_bs_read_int(bs, 4);
        if (i+1 < ptr->sampleCount) {
            ptr->sizes[i+1] = gf_bs_read_int(bs, 4);
        } else {
            //0 padding in odd sample count
            gf_bs_read_int(bs, 4);
        }
        i += 2;
        break;
    default:
        ptr->sizes[i] = gf_bs_read_int(bs, ptr->sampleSize);
        i += 1;
        break;
    }
    if (ptr->max_size < ptr->sizes[i])   // <-- BUG: reads ptr->sizes[i] after i was incremented
        ptr->max_size = ptr->sizes[i];
    ptr->total_size += ptr->sizes[i];
    ptr->total_samples++;
}
```

The bug is that after `i += 2` (in the case 4 branch), the code accesses `ptr->sizes[i]` where `i` is now `i+2`. If `sampleCount` is odd, then when `i = sampleCount - 1`, the loop body executes with `i = sampleCount - 1`, then `i += 2` makes `i = sampleCount + 1`, and then `ptr->sizes[i]` reads out of bounds (index `sampleCount + 1`).

Wait, let me trace through. The loop condition is `i < ptr->sampleCount`. 

For `sampleSize == 4`:
- If `sampleCount` is odd, say `sampleCount = 3`:
  - i=0: sizes[0]=read, i+1=1 < 3 so sizes[1]=read, i=2. Then `ptr->sizes[2]` (valid). total_samples++.
  - i=2: 2 < 3, so enter. sizes[2]=read, i+1=3 not < 3, so read padding. i=4. Then `ptr->sizes[4]` - OUT OF BOUNDS! (allocated 3 entries, indices 0-2).

So the bug is: when `sampleCount` is odd and `sampleSize == 4`, the loop reads `ptr->sizes[i]` where `i` has been incremented to `sampleCount + 1`, which is out of bounds.

Wait, let me re-check. When `i = sampleCount - 1` (last iteration for odd count):
- `i = sampleCount - 1`
- `ptr->sizes[i] = gf_bs_read_int(bs, 4)` writes to `sizes[sampleCount-1]` (valid)
- `i+1 = sampleCount`, not `< sampleCount`, so read padding
- `i += 2` → `i = sampleCount + 1`
- `ptr->sizes[i]` → `sizes[sampleCount+1]` - OUT OF BOUNDS READ

Yes! This is the heap-buffer-overflow READ 4. The `ptr->sizes[i]` access after `i += 2` reads out of bounds.

Now, this is the `stz2` box (compact sample size). The `sampleSize` is set to 4 in the "try to fix the file" path when `estSize == 0` and `(sampleCount+1)/2 == size`. 

Wait, let me re-read. The `sampleSize == 4` case in the compact path. Let me look at how `sampleSize` becomes 4.

In the stz2 path (s->type != STSZ):
```c
i = gf_bs_read_u8(bs);  // field_size
ptr->sampleCount = gf_bs_read_u32(bs);
switch (i) {
case 4:
case 8:
case 16:
    ptr->sampleSize = i;
    break;
default:
    //try to fix the file
    if (!ptr->sampleCount) {
        ptr->sampleSize = 16;
        return GF_OK;
    }
    estSize = (u32) (ptr->size) / ptr->sampleCount;
    if (!estSize && ((ptr->sampleCount+1)/2 == (ptr->size)) ) {
        ptr->sampleSize = 4;
        break;
    } else if (estSize == 1 || estSize == 2) {
        ptr->sampleSize = 8 * estSize;
    } else {
        return GF_ISOM_INVALID_FILE;
    }
}
```

So `sampleSize` can be 4 either directly from the field (case 4) or from the "fix" path. Then in the compact path:

```c
if (ptr->sampleSize==4) {
    if (ptr->sampleCount / 2 > ptr->size) {
        ... return GF_ISOM_INVALID_FILE;
    }
} else {
    ...
}
ptr->sizes = (u32 *) gf_malloc(ptr->sampleCount * sizeof(u32));
...
for (i = 0; i < ptr->sampleCount; ) {
    switch (ptr->sampleSize) {
    case 4:
        ptr->sizes[i] = gf_bs_read_int(bs, 4);
        if (i+1 < ptr->sampleCount) {
            ptr->sizes[i+1] = gf_bs_read_int(bs, 4);
        } else {
            gf_bs_read_int(bs, 4);
        }
        i += 2;
        break;
    ...
    }
    if (ptr->max_size < ptr->sizes[i])  // OOB read when i = sampleCount+1
        ptr->max_size = ptr->sizes[i];
    ptr->total_size += ptr->sizes[i];
    ptr->total_samples++;
}
```

So I need to craft an MP4 file with a `stz2` box (compact sample size) where:
- `sampleCount` is odd
- `sampleSize == 4`
- The box has enough data to pass the checks

